1
00:01:48,500 --> 00:03:03,420
don't sell as well either. That's right. Peter. Thank you very much. We need to call

2
00:03:03,420 --> 00:03:19,630
people in. So that's more than I'm willing to talk. Let me first say that I'm delighted

3
00:03:19,630 --> 00:03:28,250
to be back at the Graduate Business School at NYU, which for 22 years was my home and

4
00:03:28,250 --> 00:03:30,670
where I still feel very much at home.

5
00:03:31,650 --> 00:03:36,850
I'm delighted to see so many old friends in this audience.

6
00:03:37,690 --> 00:03:45,980
And, okay, and very grateful to my old friends and colleagues,

7
00:03:45,980 --> 00:03:51,960
Bill Goose and Dale Sand, for being willing to share this day with me here.

8
00:03:53,760 --> 00:03:58,610
And you know I'm going to break a rule of a lifetime today,

9
00:03:58,610 --> 00:04:04,910
which I learned when I was a young banker in London and my senior partner

10
00:04:04,910 --> 00:04:12,010
who was a wise old man said Mr. Drucker if you forecast either for 30 days or

11
00:04:12,010 --> 00:04:19,670
for 50 years and not for in between people have long memories and I'm going

12
00:04:19,670 --> 00:04:25,970
to forecast for 10 years or so and I only hope that you don't have long

13
00:04:25,970 --> 00:04:39,780
memory, because I think one can say with very high probability that the human resources management

14
00:04:39,780 --> 00:04:45,380
job a few years out, and I don't know whether that's five or seven or three or eight,

15
00:04:45,940 --> 00:04:51,220
but we are in the transition, will look very different from what it has been traditionally.

16
00:04:51,220 --> 00:04:59,350
First, it will not be a staff job, not even.

17
00:04:59,350 --> 00:05:05,050
Maybe in name still we are very good at maintaining labels, even though we change the contents

18
00:05:05,050 --> 00:05:06,050
of the bottle.

19
00:05:06,050 --> 00:05:13,670
It will be an operating job, which, by the way, outside of this country it has already

20
00:05:13,670 --> 00:05:18,810
become, especially in Japan.

21
00:05:18,810 --> 00:05:29,550
It will be the job that makes people's decisions rather than advises on them very heavily,

22
00:05:29,550 --> 00:05:37,220
working with the line people but not just at their elbow.

23
00:05:37,220 --> 00:05:49,430
And it will increasingly be concerned not just with hiring people and training them

24
00:05:49,430 --> 00:05:55,360
and putting them in jobs, but with the design of the job.

25
00:05:55,360 --> 00:06:00,740
In fact, I think one can say that the biggest job ahead of us

26
00:06:00,740 --> 00:06:08,300
is to look at the existing jobs and redesign and rethink them.

27
00:06:08,300 --> 00:06:12,360
And it will be very much concerned

28
00:06:12,360 --> 00:06:15,520
with strategic planning.

29
00:06:15,520 --> 00:06:24,710
In fact, after the, I would say, almost total failure

30
00:06:24,710 --> 00:06:32,340
strategic planning based on financial planning and you know in a way I came in

31
00:06:32,340 --> 00:06:40,370
at the beginning and it has not really worked after that I think we will learn

32
00:06:40,370 --> 00:06:48,390
we are beginning to learn to plan with two pillars one is marketing and one is

33
00:06:48,390 --> 00:06:55,500
people and we will learn to start out with the availability of people what

34
00:06:55,500 --> 00:07:04,090
kinds of people and what numbers, because numbers will be pretty critical, and will

35
00:07:04,090 --> 00:07:15,790
then factor this into as a major positive factor, a dynamic factor in planning

36
00:07:15,790 --> 00:07:24,860
direction and strategy increasing. And so let me say why I think these are the

37
00:07:24,860 --> 00:07:34,140
directions in which we are going. One is because, as we know, I went up to Bill

38
00:07:34,140 --> 00:07:40,080
Goose's office this morning and the offices here have not grown any bigger

39
00:07:40,080 --> 00:07:49,100
since I left. And when the three of us tried to get into Bill Goose's office, one

40
00:07:49,100 --> 00:07:55,440
of us had to stand outside. And that is a sharp change from when I was here.

41
00:07:55,440 --> 00:07:57,960
We could have three people in an office, barely.

42
00:07:58,460 --> 00:08:01,620
Now two, and you overflow.

43
00:08:02,080 --> 00:08:07,370
Because Bill now, where he should have another human being, has a computer.

44
00:08:08,410 --> 00:08:11,080
And it crowds out people.

45
00:08:12,180 --> 00:08:17,200
And he's complaining that the university still doesn't have electronic mail.

46
00:08:17,540 --> 00:08:21,320
If he ever gets it, even he won't be able to be in that office.

47
00:08:21,320 --> 00:08:38,310
as we restructure organizations around information that this changes the character of the organization

48
00:08:38,310 --> 00:08:45,660
and not just the character of information and that to the extent to which we actually

49
00:08:45,660 --> 00:08:54,780
learn to base ourselves on information we need fewer and fewer and fewer managers

50
00:08:54,780 --> 00:09:14,970
I think some of you may remember, I said it in this room a couple of years ago, that the prototype of the organization of tomorrow is unlikely to still be the military of 1870, from which we first learned the organization.

51
00:09:14,970 --> 00:09:23,380
it's going to be the symphony orchestra. And if you see, not just here, see one of

52
00:09:23,380 --> 00:09:30,160
the late Mahler symphonies, let's say the seventh, where there are 1,000 players on

53
00:09:30,160 --> 00:09:35,480
the stage. By the way, that's always been the reason why I had very limited use

54
00:09:35,480 --> 00:09:42,360
for Mahler. Haydn could do just as well with only 38 people. I've always figured

55
00:09:42,360 --> 00:09:49,920
got those 1,000 by an overkill. But still, here's the art. And here is that lowly triangle.

56
00:09:51,180 --> 00:09:58,000
And you know, if you go by our management books, that symphony orchestra should have

57
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:05,200
a chief executive conductor and four vice chairman conductors, at least four, probably

58
00:10:05,200 --> 00:10:12,520
five, and 16 senior VP conductors, and God knows how many department managers. Instead

59
00:10:12,520 --> 00:10:22,360
of achieving that lonely triangle placed directly to one conductor because both know the score

60
00:10:22,360 --> 00:10:30,120
and because that triangle takes information responsibility upward responsibility and that's

61
00:10:30,120 --> 00:10:42,700
an extreme case uh when it is not a completely valid analogy uh because the score has already

62
00:10:42,700 --> 00:10:51,270
been written and then a business reorganization we write it as we play uh when so maybe a jazz

63
00:10:51,270 --> 00:11:03,430
combo in which you uh improvise is a better analogy but still i sat in listening uh last

64
00:11:03,430 --> 00:11:12,550
what a year ago in last july to a jazz festival in colorado springs and there was a wonderful combo

65
00:11:12,550 --> 00:11:19,190
of 36 players each improvised in turn not in turn as the spirit moved him or her

66
00:11:20,870 --> 00:11:31,130
and not even a conductor and again they don't know the score but they share the information

67
00:11:31,130 --> 00:11:39,450
and so whenever we have moved towards information based organizations we have found that most

68
00:11:39,450 --> 00:11:47,050
levels of management turn out to be information relays have no decision responsibility

69
00:11:47,690 --> 00:11:56,140
very little management responsibility and become redundant and we are replacing them with

70
00:11:56,940 --> 00:12:08,780
groups of specialists like the bassoonists each playing directly to maybe not all the way to the

71
00:12:08,780 --> 00:12:17,720
top but directly with very few intermediate layers when that is I think moving very fast

72
00:12:19,580 --> 00:12:34,220
and it means a change in the way we structure first it means that we do need a central point

73
00:12:34,220 --> 00:12:42,460
in the organization where that is takes looks after people that is conscious of people

74
00:12:42,460 --> 00:12:50,300
and work with them and because we can no longer depend on layers of intermediate managers

75
00:12:50,300 --> 00:13:04,920
to note uh just the sad conductor is the one who uh runs the uh test for a new clarinetist

76
00:13:04,920 --> 00:13:14,520
himself and doesn't delegate so increasingly we will have to have some place pretty high up and

77
00:13:14,520 --> 00:13:21,720
central in the organization especially the large one which works directly with knowledge people

78
00:13:22,040 --> 00:13:31,530
each having high skill, but each having limited. But also, you know, that creates a problem.

79
00:13:33,720 --> 00:13:44,750
The Modern Symphony Orchestra was created by Gustav Mahler by a very simple method. By the way,

80
00:13:44,870 --> 00:13:54,580
it provoked the first musician's strike in history. He moved into the Vienna Philharmonic in 1896

81
00:13:54,580 --> 00:13:58,850
when it was a fifth-rate orchestra.

82
00:13:59,750 --> 00:14:04,130
And within a year, it had become the leading musical organization

83
00:14:04,130 --> 00:14:08,590
of the civilized world by a very simple device.

84
00:14:09,790 --> 00:14:13,850
Each of these musicians, and there were great gentlemen in each,

85
00:14:13,930 --> 00:14:16,850
was a professor and taught at the conservatory,

86
00:14:17,550 --> 00:14:20,070
and there were enough people easily pushed around.

87
00:14:21,160 --> 00:14:25,180
Each of them was supposed to play five evenings a week.

88
00:14:25,180 --> 00:14:33,110
And Mahana said, yes, indeed, you are on duty five evenings, but you will only play four.

89
00:14:34,090 --> 00:14:38,940
The fifth evening, you sit out in the audience and listen.

90
00:14:40,800 --> 00:14:48,080
And there is a letter on record from his greatest instrumentalist of the fifth bassoon, who

91
00:14:48,080 --> 00:14:57,680
was a very great man, in which he protested and said, I love to play the bassoon, but

92
00:14:57,680 --> 00:14:58,740
I hate music.

93
00:14:58,740 --> 00:15:06,850
Mind you, you could have been a computer expert, you know, because you get exactly the same

94
00:15:06,850 --> 00:15:13,770
letter if you force a computer man to go to a management seminar, haven't you gotten it?

95
00:15:13,770 --> 00:15:23,190
Mahler answered, by the way, I love music, but I hate the bassoon, but Mahler never used

96
00:15:23,190 --> 00:15:24,190
it.

97
00:15:24,190 --> 00:15:26,010
There are very almost no bassoon parts in Mahler.

98
00:15:26,010 --> 00:15:34,250
So and then that bassoon is called a strike, it's the last.

99
00:15:34,250 --> 00:15:41,050
But Mahler had to increase his salary 50 percent.

100
00:15:41,050 --> 00:15:47,730
And that's a funny story, but not without relevance, because you will have to make sure

101
00:15:47,730 --> 00:15:52,460
that these instrumentalists hear music.

102
00:15:52,460 --> 00:15:58,200
But if you play in an orchestra, you hear only your own instrument.

103
00:15:58,200 --> 00:16:04,040
If you are the computer specialist at Citibank, and you have a few here around, you know only

104
00:16:04,040 --> 00:16:11,980
your own computer and you really don't want to know anything else and yet the performance of the

105
00:16:12,620 --> 00:16:21,130
information-based organization will heavily depend on your knowing the score and that means somehow

106
00:16:21,130 --> 00:16:29,740
be made to hear the music and about the only way it can be done is by you're being exposed

107
00:16:29,740 --> 00:16:41,500
enough in task forces maybe in job rotation and this requires somebody who

108
00:16:41,500 --> 00:16:49,260
watches over the human organization and make sure that the people in it

109
00:16:49,260 --> 00:16:58,560
especially highly key knowledge people are exposed to the music one way or

110
00:16:58,560 --> 00:17:08,090
another, not just by getting a report, but by having to listen to it, by having to participate.

111
00:17:08,970 --> 00:17:18,120
And this is going to be increasingly important. Especially, let me say, as most of these people

112
00:17:18,120 --> 00:17:29,680
will not want to leave their specialty. And that person has no desire to become a first violin.

113
00:17:29,680 --> 00:17:35,140
All he wants to do is have more music with good bassoon parts.

114
00:17:35,820 --> 00:17:41,500
That computer specialist of yours wants the title and wants the salary,

115
00:17:41,500 --> 00:17:45,200
but he wants to keep on playing with his computer.

116
00:17:45,940 --> 00:17:49,860
And on top of that, his concept of long-range planning

117
00:17:49,860 --> 00:17:53,800
is to get in more bigger and faster computers.

118
00:17:55,020 --> 00:17:58,980
And that's basically what to him is progress.

119
00:17:58,980 --> 00:18:05,140
And so, believe me, it's a market researcher, and so is every knowledge specialist.

120
00:18:06,790 --> 00:18:16,220
And then we have to have a place that makes sure that while they are indeed become better specialists,

121
00:18:16,720 --> 00:18:25,500
they are also learning how to reach the score and to participate and take up responsibility.

122
00:18:25,720 --> 00:18:31,120
And another reason which is quite different, though not connected,

123
00:18:31,900 --> 00:18:40,410
is that the next few years are going to be very difficult for the management of people

124
00:18:40,410 --> 00:18:53,990
since the new people we are getting in are inevitably going to be profoundly disappointed

125
00:18:53,990 --> 00:19:01,100
and disenchanted and, well, I wouldn't say rebellious, but bitter.

126
00:19:03,270 --> 00:19:09,180
When you look at the younger people in your organization today,

127
00:19:09,180 --> 00:19:21,670
the people are under 40. That's now the oldest baby boomer is turning 40 this year or next.

128
00:19:22,600 --> 00:19:31,170
And when they came in, there was a vacuum. There are people here from the Chase Bank,

129
00:19:31,170 --> 00:19:39,250
so I hope they will forgive me my telling the story. I had, well, it was messed up in

130
00:19:39,250 --> 00:19:46,730
1953, and I was teaching down here, not in those new buildings, of course, we didn't

131
00:19:46,730 --> 00:19:53,510
have it, when I got the call from an old boss of mine, boss during World War II, who was

132
00:19:53,510 --> 00:20:00,300
chairman, can you all hear me, Martha, can you hear me? I was chairman of the Chase when

133
00:20:00,300 --> 00:20:08,440
it was just merging with the Bank of Manhattan, and he asked me to chair the first joint management

134
00:20:08,440 --> 00:20:15,920
meeting of the two banks, and I was greatly flattered. And then he said, you know, Peter,

135
00:20:16,340 --> 00:20:22,960
this is not because I think so highly of you. This is because nobody from either side could

136
00:20:22,960 --> 00:20:32,670
hear you. It has to be an outsider. And this was a command performance, assistant vice

137
00:20:32,670 --> 00:20:39,780
president and better. And in Princeton, and if you didn't appear, your resignation had

138
00:20:39,780 --> 00:20:48,320
been accepted, and quite a few didn't. It was not a hostile takeover, but not a friendly

139
00:20:48,320 --> 00:20:54,820
merge. Those two organizations for 100 years, they had been the chief enemies in New York

140
00:20:54,820 --> 00:21:06,640
banking, and for them to go to bed together was quite new. And I was in my 40s, I was

141
00:21:06,640 --> 00:21:17,240
44 and the next youngest man in that group of about 600 bank officers or 400 was 57 which by

142
00:21:17,240 --> 00:21:23,000
the way explains why that merger worked a few years later there wasn't anybody there anymore

143
00:21:24,740 --> 00:21:33,300
they could never really accept the other side and then no new york bank had hired anybody

144
00:21:33,300 --> 00:21:45,200
after 1929 for reasons which some of you may have heard of and uh and our schools looked the same

145
00:21:45,200 --> 00:21:54,080
way when i came in here was a wonderful faculty but within my first six or seven years practically

146
00:21:54,080 --> 00:22:00,640
all my colleagues retired they had been here since what the school hadn't hired anybody since

147
00:22:00,640 --> 00:22:09,230
the early 70s. And so it went. And so the young people came into a vacuum during a period of

148
00:22:09,230 --> 00:22:19,220
tremendous economic expansion, and they rose very fast. And now their nieces come in, or their

149
00:22:19,220 --> 00:22:26,860
cousins, and they expect the same promotion, and they cannot possibly have it, no matter

150
00:22:26,860 --> 00:22:31,080
how well the economy might be doing.

151
00:22:32,090 --> 00:22:33,550
The pipelines are full,

152
00:22:34,030 --> 00:22:35,130
which, by the way,

153
00:22:35,810 --> 00:22:39,210
may be the single most important reason

154
00:22:39,210 --> 00:22:42,810
for the tremendous entrepreneurial explosion

155
00:22:42,810 --> 00:22:43,650
in this country.

156
00:22:45,030 --> 00:22:46,550
The young people come in.

157
00:22:47,090 --> 00:22:49,750
They're being trained by Citibank or by GE

158
00:22:49,750 --> 00:22:52,490
or by Allied Chemicals

159
00:22:53,330 --> 00:22:55,410
for five years and learn something.

160
00:22:55,410 --> 00:23:21,310
And then they look and see that that assistant vice president or that director to whom they report is 28, and the assistant vice president to whom the director reports is 29, and the vice president is 32, and the senior vice president is 34, and the executive vice president, who has to go on, you know.

161
00:23:21,310 --> 00:23:27,230
and they figure out that they're all going to wait 40 years

162
00:23:27,230 --> 00:23:30,150
and then they are available to go out

163
00:23:30,150 --> 00:23:35,310
and become the marketing person for a small company.

164
00:23:35,890 --> 00:23:39,370
And believe me, whenever I sit down and talk with the people

165
00:23:39,370 --> 00:23:41,470
in entrepreneurial companies,

166
00:23:41,930 --> 00:23:47,140
they say, how come you are here and where did you start?

167
00:23:47,220 --> 00:23:49,540
Oh, we started in Security Pacific.

168
00:23:50,740 --> 00:23:53,620
Why did you leave?

169
00:23:53,620 --> 00:23:57,360
oh, the bank is fine, they treated me royally, they overpaid me.

170
00:23:57,760 --> 00:24:03,380
But, you know, I couldn't see myself being a branch manager for the next 28 years.

171
00:24:05,240 --> 00:24:14,980
And so you will have people who need to have their expectations changed.

172
00:24:16,080 --> 00:24:22,340
And that, believe me, can only be done if you restructure their job.

173
00:24:22,340 --> 00:24:40,080
In any period in which you have an acute, you know, it's the law of nature that before you can have somebody in front of a class, he or she has to have sat on their rear end in that class.

174
00:24:40,080 --> 00:24:49,160
So before, if you have a baby boom and you suddenly increase the number of kids,

175
00:24:49,720 --> 00:24:55,760
who almost tripled it, you have a shortage of teachers for 20 years,

176
00:24:55,820 --> 00:24:59,900
until those babies get to 25 years and can start teaching.

177
00:25:01,440 --> 00:25:05,060
And then you always have to make the job more.

178
00:25:05,060 --> 00:25:14,880
If you look at our school, extreme example, when I first got to be on a school board,

179
00:25:15,040 --> 00:25:23,920
elementary school had a principal and teacher. Seven years later, when I left

180
00:25:23,920 --> 00:25:28,320
the school board, there were two assistant principals and a curriculum

181
00:25:28,320 --> 00:25:33,380
coordinator and five other administrators, simply because the

182
00:25:33,380 --> 00:25:41,020
teachers were all green. No teacher, you know, that old... When we moved back into the New York

183
00:25:41,020 --> 00:25:48,160
area, that teacher who was the thought-share trader my son was, had been a

184
00:25:48,160 --> 00:25:54,620
a teacher for 30 years and there was nothing eight-year-olds could do that she hadn't been

185
00:25:54,620 --> 00:26:03,510
through. And her successor, who taught the same boys the next year in fourth grade, was straight

186
00:26:03,510 --> 00:26:12,340
off college. And, you know, eight years, nine-year-olds are easy to teach, my teacher friends

187
00:26:12,340 --> 00:26:18,080
tell me. But that poor woman, she lived next door, came over to weep on my shoulder every day

188
00:26:18,080 --> 00:26:23,680
simply because what nine-year-old kids did, everything was new to her.

189
00:26:24,180 --> 00:26:28,400
A perfectly normal kid, well-behaved kid, but still nine-year-old.

190
00:26:29,000 --> 00:26:34,540
And then she needed an assistant principal and a curriculum coordinator

191
00:26:34,540 --> 00:26:40,820
and a reading advisor, and we built those super staff in our schools,

192
00:26:40,960 --> 00:26:45,700
but not just in our schools, every place, to make the infant job easy.

193
00:26:45,700 --> 00:27:06,320
And that will no longer work, because people will have to stay on these jobs, maybe not the entrance job, but the next one, a very long time, and we will have to redesign jobs so that after five years, they're still challenged, even if they're very competent.

194
00:27:06,320 --> 00:27:38,570
And mind you, this isn't the first time. When I came in, I was at age 21. I was assistant managing editor of the big newspaper with a circulation of almost half a million. Not because I was any good, this is 1930, but because the ones who should have had that job were lying in the offices of slaves all over Europe and afterwards, in England particularly.

195
00:27:39,170 --> 00:27:47,330
afterwards were ones that just weren't any 30 year old competent men around. They didn't exist. And

196
00:27:47,330 --> 00:27:56,050
so you had to put the green kids, and there was one, into that job. But also normally a newspaper

197
00:27:56,050 --> 00:28:04,510
like this has one managing editor, assistant managing editor. We needed four of us to do that

198
00:28:04,510 --> 00:28:14,030
job because none of us had, we were not even ready to be good copy editors after a year and a half.

199
00:28:14,670 --> 00:28:23,920
So, and then one has to, when that is over, one has to learn to restructure the job. So that's

200
00:28:23,920 --> 00:28:31,970
the second challenge. And this is probably the biggest and toughest job human resources people

201
00:28:31,970 --> 00:28:41,380
faced and then after it the third one that they will have to develop policies procedures

202
00:28:41,380 --> 00:28:52,800
and compensation because our policies and procedures are largely uh refinement development

203
00:28:53,360 --> 00:29:03,900
from what we first developed to cope with unskilled blue-collar industrial workers

204
00:29:03,900 --> 00:29:13,890
Personnel management is a creation of World War I. Suddenly, enormous numbers of free industrial

205
00:29:13,890 --> 00:29:26,030
people streamed into the munitions factories in every belligerent country. And because, unlike

206
00:29:26,030 --> 00:29:33,230
the Industrial Revolution, they had substantial political clout, and nobody there risked a strike,

207
00:29:33,230 --> 00:29:42,350
in World War I, and so instead of being treated with a whip, as the predecessors had been

208
00:29:42,350 --> 00:29:48,470
treated in the 1840s, they had to be looked after.

209
00:29:49,090 --> 00:29:52,610
And this is how our personnel management was created.

210
00:29:53,390 --> 00:30:00,810
